<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="BlackjackApp.Maui.MainPage"
             Title="Blackjack"
             BackgroundColor="#2A4D00">

    <!--
        Root layer: the felt artwork, the table laid out on top of it, and -
        above everything - a single free-floating "ghost" chip image used for
        the smooth custom chip-drag (see MainPage.xaml.cs
        ChipImage_OnPanUpdated). The ghost has to live here, as a sibling of
        the table rather than inside it, so it can be placed at any absolute
        point on the page without being clipped by an ancestor's bounds.
    -->
    <Grid x:Name="RootLayout" SafeAreaEdges="None">

        <!--
            The table itself, in two layers exactly as the art was drawn:
            a full-bleed patterned backdrop, and over it the lit felt with
            its wooden lip, which is transparent below that lip so the
            backdrop shows through underneath. Both are AspectFill so they
            cover any window shape without letterboxing, and both are
            InputTransparent so they never eat a tap or swallow a chip
            drag aimed at the table above them. UpdateTableColors swaps
            both sources per variant - the kit ships a green, a red and a
            gold table, which is what the design doc's three variants want.
        -->
        <!--
            SafeAreaEdges="None" on this root so the table art stays
            full-bleed behind the notch and the home indicator. .NET 10
            changed the default: layouts now inset themselves ("Container")
            unless told otherwise, so without this the artwork would stop
            short of the screen edges on a notched iPhone and leave bars.
            The content layer below opts back in, so only the controls
            actually move inwards.
        -->
        <!--
            One opaque layer, not two translucent ones. The backdrop and the
            felt used to be separate Images stacked on top of each other, both
            carrying alpha, so every animated frame blended two full-screen
            2048x1536 layers before anything else was drawn. They are only ever
            shown in three fixed pairings, so those pairings are now baked into
            one image per variant - the same pixels, composited once at build
            time instead of sixty times a second. Being fully opaque also lets
            the compositor skip whatever is behind it.
        -->
        <Image x:Name="TableImage" Source="table_green.png" Aspect="AspectFill" InputTransparent="True"/>

        <!--
            The table, laid out the way the design doc's reference table is:
            the dealer's end across the top, open felt through the middle
            with the seats curving along its near edge, the controls down on
            the wooden rail, and a dark bar along the very bottom carrying
            the balance and the total bet.

            Deliberately NOT wrapped in a ScrollView - a card table is a
            fixed composition that should always be whole on screen, not
            something you scroll around, which is also why the app is locked
            to landscape (see Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs and
            Platforms/iOS/Info.plist). The two control strips on the rail
            keep their own horizontal ScrollViews so a narrow window
            degrades by scrolling just that row rather than clipping it.

            The felt art is drawn in perspective - dark at the dealer's end,
            lit through the middle, with the rail curving along the bottom -
            so these rows are positioned to land on the matching part of the
            picture. It can only ever be approximate, since AspectFill
            re-crops the art for whatever shape the window is.
        -->
        <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto" SafeAreaEdges="Container">

            <!--
                Row 0: the dealer's end of the table. Shoe on the left
                (where the dealing animation's cards visually come from),
                the dealer's hand centred, and on the right the table
                limits, the discard pile (where each round's played cards
                actually go instead of vanishing) and the menu.

                No panel or box around any of it - the felt art already
                separates the dealer's end from the players' by being
                darker up here, which is the job the old bordered box was
                doing badly.

                Deliberately NOT three columns. The dealer has to sit on
                the centre line of the table itself, and columns can't give
                that: with "Auto,*,Auto" the middle is only whatever is left
                between the edges, and the right-hand cluster is far wider
                than the shoe, so the dealer lands left of centre and out of
                line with the seats below. Flipping to "*,Auto,*" centres it
                but breaks something worse - an Auto column measures its
                content unconstrained, and back when the dealer's cards were a
                wrapping FlexLayout that collapsed the row to one card per line,
                making it one card wide and several tall, which starved the edge
                columns and shoved the rail and the bottom bar off screen. The
                card rows are pinned stacks now rather than wrapping layouts -
                that unconstrained measurement is exactly what froze the table
                on a deal - but this row stays one cell regardless, because
                that is also what puts the dealer on the true centre line.

                So: one cell, three overlapping children. The dealer's stack
                fills the whole width and centres its own contents, which
                both puts it on the true centre line and gives the wrapping
                FlexLayout a real width to measure against. The shoe and the
                right-hand cluster are laid over it at Start and End. They
                never actually collide - the dealer's cards are only ever a
                few hundred points wide in the middle - and the two edge
                clusters come last so they stay on top of the dealer's
                stack, which is InputTransparent anyway so it can't swallow
                a tap meant for the menu.
            -->
            <!--
                Two rows, the second of which collapses to nothing when it is
                not used. In landscape the dealer spans both and sits centred
                across the whole strip, overlaid between the shoe and the
                right-hand cluster exactly as before. In portrait there is no
                room to put three things side by side in 393pt, so the dealer
                drops into the second row underneath them. MainPage.xaml.cs
                ApplyAdaptiveMetrics moves it.
            -->
            <Grid x:Name="TopStrip" Grid.Row="0" Padding="14,6,14,0" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto">

                <VerticalStackLayout x:Name="DealerStack" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="2" Spacing="4"
                                     VerticalOptions="Start" HorizontalOptions="Fill" InputTransparent="True">
                    <Label x:Name="DealerHeadingLabel" Text="DEALER" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}" FontSize="17" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                    <!--
                        A stack, not a wrapping FlexLayout, and its height is
                        pinned from code (SizeDealerCardRow). A row whose height
                        grows when a card lands re-measures everything above it,
                        and on iOS that did not settle - which is what froze the
                        table on a deal. Long hands overlap instead of wrapping
                        onto a second line; see FanCardRow.
                    -->
                    <HorizontalStackLayout x:Name="DealerCardsLayout" Spacing="0"
                                           HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                    <Label x:Name="DealerValueLabel" Text="" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}"
                           FontSize="18" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                </VerticalStackLayout>

                <VerticalStackLayout x:Name="ShoeColumn" Grid.Row="0" Spacing="1" VerticalOptions="Start" HorizontalOptions="Start">
                    <Image x:Name="ShoeImage" Source="back_red.png" HeightRequest="62" Aspect="AspectFit"/>
                    <Label Text="SHOE" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}" TextColor="#E6F3C8"
                           FontSize="13" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                    <Label x:Name="ShoeCountLabel" Text="&#8212;" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}"
                           FontSize="15" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                </VerticalStackLayout>

                <HorizontalStackLayout x:Name="TopRightCluster" Grid.Row="0" Spacing="10" VerticalOptions="Start" HorizontalOptions="End">
                    <!-- The reference table prints its limits on a plate up here rather than tucking them under the shoe, and it's the first thing a player checks before sizing a bet. -->
                    <Border Style="{StaticResource ReadoutPlate}" Padding="11,5" VerticalOptions="Start">
                        <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0">
                            <Label Text="TABLE LIMITS" Style="{StaticResource ReadoutText}"
                                   FontSize="11" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                            <Label x:Name="TableLimitsLabel" Text="" Style="{StaticResource ReadoutText}"
                                   FontSize="15" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                        </VerticalStackLayout>
                    </Border>

                    <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="1" VerticalOptions="Start">
                        <Image x:Name="DiscardImage" Source="back_red.png" HeightRequest="62"
                               Aspect="AspectFit" Opacity="0.55"/>
                        <Label Text="DISCARD" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}" TextColor="#E6F3C8"
                               FontSize="13" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                        <Label x:Name="DiscardCountLabel" Text="0" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}"
                               FontSize="15" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>
                    </VerticalStackLayout>

                    <!-- Opens GameMenuPage - how-to-play reference and the deck/hand/variant Settings page both live behind this one entry point. The reference table keeps its small icons in this same corner. -->
                    <ImageButton x:Name="MenuButton" Source="btn_settings.png"
                                 WidthRequest="46" HeightRequest="46" Aspect="AspectFit"
                                 BackgroundColor="Transparent" Padding="0" BorderWidth="0"
                                 VerticalOptions="Start" Clicked="MenuButton_OnClicked"/>
                </HorizontalStackLayout>
            </Grid>

            <!--
                Row 1: the open felt. Round messages sit in the middle of
                it, roughly where a real table prints its "BLACKJACK PAYS
                3 TO 2" arc, and the seats hang off the bottom of this row
                so they land just above the rail.

                These labels used to sit on the same white plate as the
                balance; out here on the felt they're drawn with
                FeltHeading instead, whose drop shadow is what keeps them
                readable against the lit part of the art. They sit at the
                top of the band because the table's printing is anchored to
                the bottom of it, just above the seats.
            -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="1" Padding="14,2" RowDefinitions="*,Auto">

                <!--
                    The table's own printing - the current variant's house
                    rules, painted across the felt the way a real table has
                    them screen-printed on it (see MainPage.xaml.cs
                    TableRulesDrawable, and IGameVariant.TableRules for the
                    wording). First child of the row so it sits under the
                    cards and messages, exactly as printing on a real table
                    sits under whatever is dealt on top of it, and
                    InputTransparent so it can't intercept a chip drop.
                -->
                <GraphicsView x:Name="TableRulesView" Grid.Row="0" InputTransparent="True"/>

                <VerticalStackLayout Grid.Row="0" VerticalOptions="Start" HorizontalOptions="Center" Spacing="2">
                    <Label x:Name="ResultLabel" Text="" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}"
                           TextColor="#FFC400" FontSize="21"
                           HorizontalOptions="Center" HorizontalTextAlignment="Center"/>
                    <Label x:Name="WarTotalLabel" Text="" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}"
                           TextColor="#7FD4FF" FontSize="18" HorizontalOptions="Center" IsVisible="False"/>
                    <Label x:Name="TotalResultLabel" Text="" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}"
                           TextColor="#FFC400" FontSize="18" HorizontalOptions="Center" IsVisible="False"/>
                </VerticalStackLayout>

                <!--
                    Player hand slots - one per active hand (1-5, chosen in
                    Settings). Each slot's cards/chip/bet/value views are
                    built at runtime in code-behind (see MainPage.xaml.cs
                    CreateHandSlotView) since the count varies; tapping a
                    slot before dealing selects it as the current betting
                    target, and whichever slot is currently being played
                    gets a highlighted border.

                    AlignItems="End" bottoms the seats out against the rail,
                    which is what lets ApplyHandSlotArc bend the row into
                    the table's curve purely with bottom margins. It has to
                    be margins rather than TranslationY: the chip-drag
                    hit-test reads layout Bounds (see GetAbsolutePosition),
                    which a render transform wouldn't move.

                    In its own Auto row rather than sharing one cell with
                    the message above it, which used to let a long message -
                    the insurance prompt, say - print straight across the
                    seats whenever the felt got short.
                -->
                <!--
                    NOT inside a ScrollView. It was, briefly, to stop five
                    seats wrapping onto a second line - and that made the
                    whole row vanish: a FlexLayout given no height constraint
                    by a horizontal ScrollView measures to zero, so every seat
                    was laid out with no height at all. This is the third time
                    FlexLayout measurement has bitten this file (see also
                    HandSlotCardsWidth being pinned, and the dealer's cards
                    collapsing inside an Auto column), and the lesson each
                    time is the same: never hand a FlexLayout an unconstrained
                    dimension and expect a sensible answer.

                    Wrap is back because a second line is a far smaller
                    problem than no seats. It is also now mostly unreachable:
                    compact metrics narrow a seat to ~140pt on a phone, so
                    five fit across any current iPhone in landscape.
                -->
                <FlexLayout x:Name="PlayerHandsLayout" Grid.Row="1" Direction="Row" Wrap="Wrap"
                            JustifyContent="Center" AlignItems="End" VerticalOptions="End"/>
            </Grid>

            <!--
                Row 2: the wooden rail. Chips on the left, where a player's
                own stack would sit, and the action buttons on the right,
                which is where the reference table puts them.

                The two never actually compete for the space - the chip row
                is only up between rounds and the action buttons only during
                one (see SetRoundInProgress / RefreshActionButtonVisibility).

                Betting supports two interactions side by side: a custom
                smooth drag (press and drag a chip - a free-floating "ghost"
                copy of it follows your finger 1:1 the whole way, escaping
                this ScrollView's clipping via RootLayout/DragGhostImage at
                the very bottom of this page - see MainPage.xaml.cs
                ChipImage_OnPanUpdated) onto the hand slot you want to bet
                with, and tap-to-bet (tap a chip to "hold" it - see
                ChipImage_OnTapped - then tap a hand slot to place it there;
                a fallback for anywhere a drag gesture is awkward). Both
                paths route through the same PlaceChipOnHandAsync so they
                stay perfectly consistent. Each Image's ClassId carries its
                dollar denomination, since Image has no CommandParameter,
                and the chips have to stay Images specifically -
                ChipImage_OnTapped's de-highlight pass walks this layout
                with Children.OfType<Image>(), so Clear/All In being Buttons
                is what keeps them out of it. They aren't chips, so dragging
                doesn't apply to them anyway.
            -->
            <Grid x:Name="RailGrid" Grid.Row="2" Padding="14,2,14,2" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="12">

                <!--
                    NOT in a ScrollView, deliberately. A ScrollView's own pan
                    gesture wins over a child's PanGestureRecognizer on iOS and
                    Android, so while this row scrolled, a chip could be
                    dragged with a mouse on Windows but never with a finger on
                    a phone - the gesture was swallowed before it reached the
                    chip. MainPage.xaml.cs SizeChipTray shrinks the chips to
                    fit the width instead of scrolling them.
                -->
                <FlexLayout x:Name="ChipButtonsLayout" Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Direction="Row" Wrap="Wrap"
                            JustifyContent="Center" AlignItems="Center" VerticalOptions="Center">
                        <Image Source="chip_1.png" ClassId="1" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_5.png" ClassId="5" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_10.png" ClassId="10" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_25.png" ClassId="25" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_50.png" ClassId="50" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_100.png" ClassId="100" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_500.png" ClassId="500" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_1000.png" ClassId="1000" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_5000.png" ClassId="5000" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Image Source="chip_10000.png" ClassId="10000" WidthRequest="54" HeightRequest="54" Aspect="AspectFit">
                            <Image.GestureRecognizers>
                                <PanGestureRecognizer PanUpdated="ChipImage_OnPanUpdated"/>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="ChipImage_OnTapped"/>
                            </Image.GestureRecognizers>
                        </Image>
                        <Button x:Name="ClearBetButton" Text="Clear" Style="{StaticResource ChipTrayButton}"
                                Margin="12,2,2,2" Clicked="ClearBetButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <Button x:Name="AllInButton" Text="All In" Style="{StaticResource ChipTrayButton}"
                                Background="{StaticResource GoldButtonBrush}" TextColor="{StaticResource GoldInk}"
                                Margin="2" Clicked="AllInButton_OnClicked"/>
                </FlexLayout>

                <!--
                    Hit/Stand/Double/Split are the kit's own button art
                    (ImageButton, since the label is painted into the PNG);
                    the rest are our labels, so they're drawn with the
                    gradient styles in Casino.xaml, whose stops were sampled
                    out of those same four PNGs so both kinds sit in one
                    palette. Deal reuses Hit's green because the two are
                    never on screen together - Deal only shows between
                    rounds, Hit only during one (see SetRoundInProgress).
                -->
                <FlexLayout x:Name="ActionButtonsLayout" Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Direction="Row" Wrap="Wrap"
                            JustifyContent="End" AlignItems="Center">
                        <Button x:Name="DealButton" Text="DEAL" Style="{StaticResource GreenButton}"
                                WidthRequest="112" FontSize="22" Clicked="DealButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <!-- No IsEnabled="False" here - RefreshActionButtonVisibility (code-behind) is the sole gatekeeper now, hiding each one entirely via IsVisible rather than graying it out, so it also needs to start out enabled. -->
                        <ImageButton x:Name="HitButton" Source="btn_hit.png" WidthRequest="106" HeightRequest="48"
                                     Aspect="AspectFit" BackgroundColor="Transparent" Padding="0" BorderWidth="0"
                                     IsVisible="False" Clicked="HitButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <ImageButton x:Name="StandButton" Source="btn_stand.png" WidthRequest="106" HeightRequest="48"
                                     Aspect="AspectFit" BackgroundColor="Transparent" Padding="0" BorderWidth="0"
                                     IsVisible="False" Clicked="StandButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <ImageButton x:Name="DoubleButton" Source="btn_double.png" WidthRequest="106" HeightRequest="48"
                                     Aspect="AspectFit" BackgroundColor="Transparent" Padding="0" BorderWidth="0"
                                     IsVisible="False" Clicked="DoubleButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <ImageButton x:Name="SplitButton" Source="btn_split.png" WidthRequest="106" HeightRequest="48"
                                     Aspect="AspectFit" BackgroundColor="Transparent" Padding="0" BorderWidth="0"
                                     IsVisible="False" Clicked="SplitButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <!-- War Blackjack only - shown after a hand's War card beats the dealer's, replacing the normal action buttons for that moment. -->
                        <Button x:Name="PressWarButton" Text="PRESS" Style="{StaticResource GoldButton}"
                                WidthRequest="104" IsVisible="False" Clicked="PressWarButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <Button x:Name="CashOutWarButton" Text="CASH OUT" Style="{StaticResource GreenButton}"
                                WidthRequest="118" FontSize="18" IsVisible="False" Clicked="CashOutWarButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <!-- Standard Blackjack only - shown once, right after the opening deal, whenever the dealer's up card is an Ace (see MainPage.PromptForInsurance). -->
                        <Button x:Name="InsuranceButton" Text="INSURE" Style="{StaticResource BlueButton}"
                                WidthRequest="108" IsVisible="False" Clicked="InsuranceButton_OnClicked"/>
                        <Button x:Name="DeclineInsuranceButton" Text="NO INSURANCE" Style="{StaticResource RedButton}"
                                WidthRequest="146" FontSize="17" IsVisible="False" Clicked="DeclineInsuranceButton_OnClicked"/>
                </FlexLayout>
            </Grid>

            <!--
                Row 3: the dark strip along the bottom of the reference
                table - balance on the left, what's currently at risk on the
                right, and the variant's name between them. It sits over the
                part of the felt art that's already black below the rail, so
                it reads as part of the table rather than as a bar laid on
                top of it.

                Both figures used to live on white plates up on the felt;
                down here they stay legible without covering any of it, and
                each one's caption means the numbers themselves no longer
                have to carry a "Balance:"/"Total Wagered:" prefix (see
                UpdateBalanceText / UpdateTotalWageredText).
            -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="3" BackgroundColor="#B3000000" Padding="18,3" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="12">

                <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Column="0" Spacing="8" VerticalOptions="Center">
                    <Image Source="icon_coin.png" WidthRequest="22" HeightRequest="22"
                           Aspect="AspectFit" VerticalOptions="Center"/>
                    <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0">
                        <Label Text="BALANCE" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}" TextColor="#E6F3C8" FontSize="11"/>
                        <Label x:Name="BalanceLabel" Text="$1,000" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}" FontSize="22"/>
                    </VerticalStackLayout>
                </HorizontalStackLayout>

                <Label x:Name="VariantLabel" Grid.Column="1" Text="Standard Blackjack"
                       Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}" TextColor="#FFC400" FontSize="15"
                       VerticalOptions="Center" HorizontalOptions="Center"/>

                <VerticalStackLayout Grid.Column="2" Spacing="0" HorizontalOptions="End" VerticalOptions="Center">
                    <Label Text="TOTAL BET" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}" TextColor="#E6F3C8"
                           FontSize="11" HorizontalOptions="End"/>
                    <Label x:Name="CurrentBetLabel" Text="$0" Style="{StaticResource FeltHeading}"
                           FontSize="22" HorizontalOptions="End"/>
                </VerticalStackLayout>
            </Grid>
        </Grid>

        <!--
            The drag ghost itself - a plain, input-transparent copy of
            whichever chip is being dragged. Positioned purely via
            TranslationX/TranslationY (see GetAbsolutePosition in the
            code-behind), never via layout, so it can be placed at any
            absolute point on the page. Hidden and fully transparent until a
            drag actually starts.
        -->
        <Image x:Name="DragGhostImage" WidthRequest="58" HeightRequest="58" Aspect="AspectFit"
               HorizontalOptions="Start" VerticalOptions="Start"
               InputTransparent="True" IsVisible="False" Opacity="0" ZIndex="999"/>
    </Grid>
</ContentPage>
